CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 41 ----------
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "What is a monolithic kernel and what are its three properties?", "All operating system services run in kernel mode in one address space, calling each other as functions. Fast (no boundary crossing between components); fragile (a driver bug can corrupt the page cache, because there is no isolation within one address space); and large — Linux is over 30 million lines, mostly drivers."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Do loadable kernel modules provide isolation?", "No. They still run in kernel mode with full privilege. Modules are a packaging improvement, not an isolation one, and people often assume otherwise."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "What is a microkernel, and what are its two genuine wins?", "Only address spaces, threads, scheduling and IPC in kernel mode; filesystems, network stacks and drivers are user-mode servers communicating by messages. Wins: a crashed driver is a restartable process that cannot corrupt what it was not given; and verifiability — seL4, about 10,000 lines, has a machine-checked proof of functional correctness."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "What is the microkernel's cost, and what reduced it?", "An operation that was a function call becomes several IPC round trips, each with a context switch and a boundary crossing. Mach (CMU, 1980s) was notably slow; L4 (Liedtke, 1993) cut IPC cost by an order of magnitude and made the argument much closer."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Summarise the 1992 Tanenbaum–Torvalds debate accurately.", "Tanenbaum posted 'LINUX is obsolete' on comp.os.minix, arguing microkernels were the settled research direction and that tying Linux to x86 was short-sighted. Torvalds, then 22, replied that Linux was portable in practice, worked, and was free — that theoretical elegance shipping nothing loses to practical code that runs."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Assess that debate thirty years on.", "Both were right about different things. Tanenbaum was right about reliability and security, borne out by the history of kernel vulnerabilities. Torvalds was right that Linux would win, comprehensively. And microkernel ideas moved rather than lost — FUSE, VFIO and DPDK, gVisor, eBPF and seL4 are all microkernel-flavoured retrofits."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Place Linux, Windows NT, XNU, Android and unikernels.", "Linux: monolithic with modules, stable interface at the syscall boundary. NT: a hybrid whose graphics moved <em>into</em> the kernel in NT 4.0 for speed, at a real cost in stability; stable interface at <code>ntdll.dll</code>. XNU: Mach plus BSD, both in kernel mode, now pushing drivers out with DriverKit. Android: Linux kernel with Bionic, ART, Binder IPC, and one UID per application. Unikernels: no user/kernel split at all, application and OS compiled into one binary."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Why is a container escape a consequence of monolithic design?", "One kernel with 30 million lines of attack surface, shared by every container, called into directly. That is the entire argument for gVisor, Kata and Firecracker, each of which is a different point on this spectrum."),
("node-41", "Node 41 · Kernel architectures", "Map the microkernel debate onto microservices, with numbers.", "A monolith is one address space with function calls: fast, simple to deploy, no fault containment. Microservices are separate processes exchanging messages: independently deployable, failures contained, and every internal call becomes ~200 µs instead of ~20 ns — four orders of magnitude, exactly the microkernel's IPC penalty. The likely resolution is the same: mostly monolithic, with boundaries where isolation earns its cost."),
# ---------- Node 42 ----------
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "What happened in 1936, 1945, 1947 and 1948–49?", "Turing's universal machine paper; the EDVAC draft describing the stored-program design; the transistor at Bell Labs; and the first stored-program executions — the Manchester Baby on 21 June 1948 and EDSAC on 6 May 1949."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "Name the four systems from 1961–65 and what each contributed.", "CTSS (MIT, 1961, Corbató): interactive time-sharing and the multi-level feedback queue. Atlas (Manchester, 1962, Kilburn): interrupts with priorities, extracodes, a Supervisor, and demand-paged virtual memory. System/360 (IBM, 1964): one architecture across a line, the 8-bit byte, and JCL. Multics (1965): protection rings, a hierarchical filesystem, dynamic linking, security as a goal."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "Why is 1973 the decisive year for Unix?", "It was rewritten in C. An operating system in a high-level language can be moved to a new machine by writing a compiler, and portability — not design — is what actually spread it."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "How did an antitrust decree shape modern computing?", "A 1956 consent decree barred AT&T from selling computer products, so Unix was licensed to universities for a nominal fee with source. A generation learned operating systems by reading it."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "What did 4.2BSD (1983) contribute, and what else happened that year?", "The socket API and TCP/IP — which is why the internet speaks Unix. The same year Stallman announced GNU, which produced GCC, Emacs and the utilities but never finished its kernel."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "Name the three things that happened to Linux in 1991–92.", "Torvalds's 25 August 1991 post and version 0.01 in September; relicensing under the GPL in 1992, which made contribution safe; and the Tanenbaum debate. Also 1992: AT&T's lawsuit against BSDi put the BSDs under a legal cloud for two years — exactly the window in which Linux gathered its community."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "Name five things from 2003–2009 and what each responded to.", "Linux 2.6 with the O(1) scheduler and NPTL (2003); nginx (2004), written against C10K; VT-x and AMD-V (2005), making virtualisation practical; EC2 (2006), changing the economics of computing; cgroups merged (2008) from Google; and Node.js at JSConf EU (November 2009)."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "What was Docker's actual contribution in March 2013?", "Not the kernel features — namespaces since 2002, cgroups since 2008, union filesystems for years. The image format, the registry, and the developer experience: making an assembly of existing kernel features usable in an afternoon. That is a genuine and sufficient contribution."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "Why is January 2018 a landmark date?", "Meltdown and Spectre: the first time a processor optimisation forced a coordinated redesign across every operating system, browser and cloud simultaneously."),
("node-42", "Node 42 · History", "State the three patterns in this history.", "The ideas were essentially finished by 1970 and everything since is engineering, scale and economics. Licensing and litigation decided outcomes at least as much as design. And simple things that ship beat elegant things that do not — Unix over Multics, Linux over Hurd, Docker over seven prior container systems."),
# ---------- Node 43 ----------
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Trace a mouse click from the contact to your React handler.", "Contact closes → USB controller interrupt → kernel input subsystem writes to <code>/dev/input/eventN</code> → the display server wakes from <code>epoll_wait</code>, is scheduled, and routes it → over a Unix socket to the browser UI process → IPC to the owning renderer process (a real process because of Site Isolation) → the renderer's event loop takes it as a task, hit-tests the layout tree, constructs a MouseEvent and dispatches it → React's root listener routes it to <code>onClick</code>."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Trace the server side from packet arrival to your route handler.", "NIC DMAs the packet and raises MSI-X → driver top half schedules NET_RX softirq → softirq walks netfilter (Service IP rewritten to a pod IP), IP and TCP, appending to a socket buffer → the wait-queue callback pushes the descriptor onto epoll's ready list → your process is marked runnable, scheduled subject to its cgroup, and context-switched in → <code>epoll_wait</code> returns in the poll phase → libuv calls back → <code>read()</code> into a Buffer outside the V8 heap → HTTP parser → V8 → your handler."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Where exactly does the event loop architecture pay for itself in a request?", "At the <code>await</code> on the database: the function suspends, a promise is returned, the query is written to a socket, and the loop serves other connections instead of a thread sitting blocked."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Trace a query inside Postgres, in mechanism terms.", "A pooled connection (because Postgres is process-per-connection); a backend wakes; the planner chooses using cost constants encoding disk physics; execution reads from <code>shared_buffers</code> (a shared mapping), falling through to the page cache and then to a real disk read during which the backend is in D state; shared structures are protected by lightweight locks that are futexes; a write appends to the WAL and <code>fsync</code>s at commit — the largest cost in the transaction."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Trace the response from <code>res.json</code> to photons.", "<code>JSON.stringify</code> runs synchronously, blocking every connection in the process; the write may return short, registering for writability; short-lived objects are scavenged in under a millisecond; packets traverse back through interrupt, softirq, TCP, the browser's network process and IPC to the renderer; the promise resolves as a microtask; React schedules a render in a lane, yielding every ~5 ms; at the next frame boundary rAF, style, layout, compositing and paint run; tiles go to the GPU; the display controller DMAs the framebuffer out sixty times a second."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "What is the actual value of knowing the forty-five-step trace?", "'The API is slow' is not a diagnosis. The list is the complete set of places the time can be, which turns guessing into enumeration."),
("node-43", "Node 43 · The whole lifecycle", "Trace the deployment lifecycle from commit to serving traffic.", "Editor write to the page cache; content-addressed Git objects; TLS push; a CI runner (a resident monitor) picks up the job in a container; <code>npm ci</code> creates a hundred thousand inodes and compiles addons against that image's libc; a bundler links modules; each Dockerfile instruction becomes an overlayfs layer; layers push to a registry by digest; the scheduler places pods by requests; the kubelet pulls, assembles the overlay, writes cgroup files, clones with namespace flags, wires a veth, pivot_roots, mounts <code>/proc</code>, sets the UID, drops capabilities, installs seccomp and execs; the dynamic linker maps libc and addons; pages fault in; V8 restores from a snapshot; the process listens and then blocks in <code>epoll_wait</code>; the readiness probe passes and kube-proxy programs netfilter; the old pod gets SIGTERM concurrently with endpoint removal."),
# ---------- Node 44 ----------
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "Name the six recurring forces.", "Sharing, protection, indirection, latency, the boundary, and compatibility."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "What are the only three answers to a sharing problem?", "Time-slice it, space-partition it, or queue for it — which are the scheduler, virtual memory, and every queue in your stack."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "State the protection rule that recurs at every layer.", "Enforcement must be below the level the protected party can reach. It is why the mode bit is hardware, why WebAssembly bounds-checks in the runtime, why eBPF has a verifier, and why Site Isolation moved to process boundaries."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "Name eight indirections and the two questions to ask about a new one.", "Virtual addresses, file descriptors, inodes, PIDs, namespaces, the VFS, sonames and the GOT, and Service IPs. Ask: what does it let the system change without telling anyone, and where is its cache?"),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "Name the three techniques for hiding latency and the two problems they all create.", "Cache, buffer, overlap. Every one creates staleness (what if the cached copy is wrong?) and durability (what if we crash before the buffer drains?) — which is why cache invalidation and <code>fsync</code> are hard in the same way."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "Give six fossils in modern computing and their causes.", "The 8-bit byte (System/360); 4 KB pages (the VAX, 1977); the 1024 descriptor default (<code>select</code>'s bitmap); port 1024 as the privilege line; the Unix epoch and 2038; and <code>SIGSEGV</code> named for a memory scheme no longer used. Interfaces outlive their reasons because the cost of change is borne by everyone and the benefit by no one."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "What is the seventh force, not in the list?", "Licensing, litigation and economics — the AT&T decree, the GPL, the BSD lawsuit, the CDDL keeping ZFS out of the kernel, and EC2's pricing model. None of it is in the mechanisms, and all of it determined which mechanisms you use."),
("node-44", "Node 44 · The six forces", "What six questions should you ask about a mechanism you have never seen?", "Which resource is shared and how? What stops one user harming another, and is enforcement below their reach? What is the name and what is the real thing, and where is the translation cached? Which slow thing is hidden, and what goes stale or gets lost? Which boundary is crossed, and what is the batching story? And what is the way it is only because of something in 1977?"),
]
